And I can be the &#39;quote-guy&#39; and give you this piece of his bio on the main SotM site. :P

&quot;Eventually, Graves was hired by a secret security force, but as part of his contract, he was chemically and mechanically enhanced, making him even more of a colossal force to be reckoned with. He was outfitted with a massive helmet resembling the front of a large train, which led to Graves being called &quot;Fright Train&quot;.&quot;

[quote="Spiff"]

So if it&#39;s a helmet, it would go on his shoulders.&nbsp; But it&#39;s never on his shoulders, it&#39;s always on his chest, which would make you think he&#39;s leaning forward.&nbsp; But I&#39;m pretty sure Adam knows how to draw necks on characters who are leaning forward, of which Fright Train seems to have none, so in my mind, the mystery is still decidedly un-solved.

[/quote]

For me, I am kinda thinking it is less &quot;helmet&quot; in the traditional sense, and more like very large ski goggles.&nbsp; This would nicely explain the Train Gaurd resting not only on his face/head but also on his chest.&nbsp; 

 Edit: Actually, we can see exactly how his Helmet functions based off the art found in his Bio. The Helmet is attached to two tracks that are strapped around his shoulders and back. You can see gears on either side of the Train Guard that act as the locking/movement mechanism. It seems like Fright Train can move his helmet into an up position, protecting his face, or in a down position, probably affording him better vision and less strain (if any at all) on his shoulders. It is totally more of a &quot;mask&quot; than it is a &quot;helmet&quot;, as it does not encase Fright Trains entire head, just his face. The back of his head is left exposed with the Train Guard in the &quot;up&quot; position. 

&nbsp;

Personally, I think his physical structure was altered in a way that he can bend his head down&nbsp;without great effort, maybe rotating his shoulders around to lower his head.

&nbsp;

(I mean this in the best possible way:) What&#39;s especially funny to me is that, in actual comics, we see women in impossible poses that contort their bodies to ensure certain body parts are visible to the&nbsp;(generally male) reader, but we don&#39;t actually concern ourselves with that. But one guy can bend his head down and we&#39;re confused and asking questions about his anatomy.  :sunglasses:
